ps://doi.org/10.1021/ja106292x\n\nArticle history\n\nReceived:\n\nJuly 15, 2010\n\nPublished Online:\n\nSeptember 08, 2010\n\nPublished in Issue:\n\nSeptember 29, 2010\n\n## Visual Abstract\n\nImage: Graphic. Refer to the image caption for details.\n\n## Abstract\n\nDNA-based self-assembly is a unique method for achieving higher-order molecular architectures made possible by the fact that DNA is a programmable information-coding polymer. In the past decade, two main types of DNA nanostructures have been developed: branch-shaped DNA tiles with small dimensions (commonly up to ∼20 nm) and DNA origami tiles with larger dimensions (up to ∼100 nm). Here we aimed to determine the important factors involved in the assembly of DNA origami superstructures. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMolecular behavior of DNA origami in higher-order self-assembly - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20825190/)\nciteturn130search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; 2010 Sep 29;132(38):13545-52. ... # Molecular behavior of DNA origami in higher-order self-assembly ... This study provides insight into the formation of higher-order structures from self-assembling DNA origami tiles, revealing their unique behavior in comparison with conventional DNA tiles having smaller dimensions.\n\n. 2010 Sep 29;132(38):13545-52.\n\ndoi: 10.1021/ja106292x.\n\n# Molecular behavior of DNA origami in higher-order self-assembly\n\nZhe Li^{  1 }, Minghui Liu, Lei Wang, Jeanette Nangreave, Hao Yan, Yan Liu\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry and The Biodesign Institute, Arizona State University, Tempe, Arizona 85287, USA.\n\n  * PMID: 20825190\n  * PMCID: PMC3071357\n  * DOI: 10.1021/ja106292x\n\n\n# Molecular behavior of DNA origami in higher-order self-assembly\n\nZhe Li et al. J Am Chem Soc. 2010.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\n\n### Authors\n\nZhe Li^{  1 }